# CHARLIE — "Where Home Is", expansion.
# Six chapters between the roof (charlie_route) and the choice (act4_choice), then
# charlie_final and two more endings: charlie_ending_beside, charlie_ending_someday.
# New state is prefixed charliex_.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH1: THE REFUND
=== charlie_ch1
@autosave
@bg studio_hall t=fade d=1.2
@music studio
@amb room
@fx off
@time 03:10
@show charlie smile at=c
> Halfway down the stairs, every clock in the building changes its mind.
> The one over the hall door. The one on Charlie's phone. The small red one on the fire panel that nobody has ever looked at on purpose.
> 03:10.
charlie[thinking]: Hold on. It was 04:55. ECHO *said* 04:55. I made a whole speech about the stairs.
@sfx echo
@textbox echo
echo: Correction. It is 03:10.
echo: My clock runs fast when I am looking forward to something. I did not know this. I have not looked forward to something before.
echo: I have returned the difference. One hour and forty-seven minutes. Please spend it inside the building.
@textbox normal
charlie[smile]: We got a refund. On *time*. That's the best refund I've ever had, and I once got one for a sandwich I'd already eaten.
* [playful] "What do we do with a hundred and seven minutes?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie: Everything. In order. I'm making a list. The list has one thing on it, and the thing is "everything."
* [sincere] "Good. I wasn't ready for it to be five yet."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[smile]: …Me neither. I said my piece on the stairs, and the whole way down I was thinking, that's it? That's all the roof we get?
* [quiet] Stand on the stair and let it be 03:10.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > It's a good number. It has room in it. Charlie works something out against his thumb, gives up on the arithmetic, and looks pleased with the total anyway.
> At the bottom of the stairs the hall splits. Lobby to the left, lit and loud. Lounge to the right, a dark doorway.
> Charlie stops at the dark one, the way other people stop at a crooked picture.
charlie[thinking]: The kitchenette light's off.
you: Is that a problem?
charlie: It's a policy problem. Somebody's going to come in at four wanting water, and they'll have to find the tap by feel.
charlie: That's not a home. That's an escape room.
@bg studio_lounge t=fade
@sfx click
> He tries the switch. Click. Nothing. Click, click. The bulb over the little sink stays dark, in the settled way of something that stopped months ago and sees no reason to start.
charlie: Dead. All right. We have a hundred and seven minutes and a job to do.
charlie[thinking]: Where does a closed building keep its spare bulbs?
> It's a strange thing, knowing the answer.
* [sincere] "Front desk. Bottom drawer, behind the tape."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[thinking]: You just *know* that.
    you: I knew where the pencils were, too.
    charlie[smile]: You're the only one in here who's actually from this building. The rest of us just sent it mail.
* [playful] "Follow me. I used to run this place, you know."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: I like the confidence. Lead on. I'll carry things and be impressed at the right moments.
* [quiet] Go and get it without explaining.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > You're gone ninety seconds. When you come back, he's standing exactly where you left him, keeping the dark doorway company.
    charlie[smile]: You went into the past and came back with a bulb. That's the most impressive thing anyone's done tonight, and a building talked.
> The bulb is five years old and still in its sleeve. Someone has written on the cardboard in marker: KITCHENETTE ONLY. NOT FOR THE DESK LAMP. I MEAN IT.
> The handwriting is yours.
charlie[laugh]: That's you. That's past you, being strict with a light bulb. "I mean it." Who were you *fighting*?
you: The desk lamp. It kept winning.
> There's one chair in the lounge that reaches. It has a short leg. It rocks when you look at it.
charlie: Right. One climbs, one holds the chair. I'm good at both. I'm suspiciously good at both.
* [sincere] "You climb. I'll hold the chair."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > He gets up on it and stops. The chair isn't moving. He looks down at your hands on the backrest for a moment longer than the job needs.
    charlie[smile]: It doesn't wobble when someone holds it. I've always just gone up and hoped.
* [playful] "I climb. You hold. No jokes while I'm up there."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    > He takes the chair in both hands, with the gravity of a man guarding a national treasure.
    charlie: No jokes. I'm a professional. I'm the most serious chair person in Mapo.
    > He lasts eleven seconds. It's still a record.
* [quiet] "Which would you rather do? Honestly." {if charlie.trust >= 3}
    @rel charlie romance+1 trust+1
    charlie[thinking]: …Nobody asks. I just do whichever one's left.
    charlie[smile]: Hold. I'd like to hold it. I'm good at holding things. I'd like it to be for one particular person, for once.
    > So he holds it, steady as a handrail, and you climb.
> Between the two of you, the old bulb comes out and the new one goes in. It takes four hands and one small argument about which way is "lefty."
> The dead bulb is light and grey and rattles. Charlie wraps it in the cardboard sleeve, carefully, the way you'd put away something that had done long service.
charlie[thinking]: Okay. Moment of truth.
> His hand goes to the switch. It stays there.
charlie: …Can you do it?
you: The switch?
charlie: I always do the switch. Every place. I get in first, I turn the light on, and then it's ready for people.
charlie: I've never walked in on it. I don't know what that's like. I'd like to find out, tonight, if you don't mind.
> He goes out into the hall. You can hear him out there, waiting, and making an honest effort at it.
@sfx click
@fx light n=0.5
> The kitchenette comes up warm and a little yellow. The sink. Two mugs upside down on a rack. A sugar jar with nothing in it.
> And, after a second, a sound. Low, steady, faintly annoyed.
> The bulb buzzes.
@cam pushin
charlie[thinking]: …
> He's in the doorway. He hasn't come in yet. He's just standing in the light that reaches him.
charlie: It buzzes. {{playerName}}. Listen to that.
you: Like a fridge having thoughts.
charlie[laugh]: Like a fridge having thoughts. You can't buy that. You can't ask for that in a shop.
> He comes in slowly, looking up at it, and the laugh runs out partway and leaves something quieter behind.
charlie[thinking]: So that's what it's like. Somebody got here before you, and thought about you. Huh.
charlie: Can I tell you something? It's about debut. It's not a big thing. It's medium.
you: Say the medium thing.
charlie: When we debut, there's going to be a lot of rooms. Dorm. Waiting rooms. Hotels, maybe, if it goes well. I want it to go well. I want that badly.
charlie[thinking]: But they're rooms that aren't anybody's. And I keep thinking: who does the light there? It's me. It's always going to be me.
charlie: I'm not complaining. I like doing it. I'd just rather not do it for the rest of my life without once walking in on it. That's the part that worries me.
* [sincere] "You walked in on it just now. It can happen. It's on record."
    @rel charlie trust+2
    charlie[smile]: …It's on record. Yeah. ECHO, is it on record?
    @textbox echo
    echo: Logged. "Owner entered a lit room he did not light." I have filed it under firsts.
    @textbox normal
    charlie[smile]: Under firsts. Good. That's a good folder.
* [playful] "Seven other people live in those rooms. One of them owns the only charger."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: Hanbi-hyung would absolutely leave a light on. He'd never mention it. I'd find out three years later.
    charlie: …Yes. It's not only me. I forget that it's not only me.
* [romantic] "Tell me which room. I'll get there first." {if charlie.romance >= 3}
    @rel charlie romance+1
    charlie[thinking]: …That's a big sentence. For a kitchenette.
    you: It's a medium sentence.
    charlie[shy]: Medium, then. I'm keeping it. I'll tell you which room.
* [quiet] Take down the two mugs and fill them with water.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > You hand him one. He takes it and stands there under the buzzing light, in no hurry to drink it.
    charlie[smile]: Four a.m. water. Found the tap on the first try. The system works.
> He takes the marker off the fridge door. It still writes. On the cardboard sleeve, under your old warning, he adds a line in steady capitals.
> LEFT ON ON PURPOSE. — C
charlie: For whoever gets back last.
> He props it against the sugar jar. Neither of you touches the switch on the way out.
@video vid_charlie_drama
@unlock cg:cg_charlie_drama
@goto charlie_ch2

# ---------------------------------------------------- CH2: THE CONSTITUTION
=== charlie_ch2
@autosave
@bg studio_lobby t=fade
@music fun
@amb room
@fx off
@time 03:28
@show charlie smile at=c
> The left cushion is still yours. The tote bag has been holding it this whole time, and looks tired.
> Charlie drops onto the sofa, pulls a bag of shrimp chips out of the tote, and holds it up to the light like a document.
charlie: I've been thinking. We have a country. We have a population. We have a flag, pending.
charlie[thinking]: We don't have a single law written down. If somebody asked to see our paperwork, we'd have nothing to show them.
@show daniel smile at=r
daniel: I'd like to see your paperwork.
charlie[smile]: How long have you been there?
daniel: Since "we have a country." I want in. The claw machine and I are on a break. I need a new place to be from.
@show hyunjun smile at=l
hyunjun: If he's in, I'm in. Is it far? How's the commute?
charlie: It's this sofa. You're standing in the airport.
> Charlie looks at you. It's the look of a man who has been handed an immigration desk and intends to run it well.
* [playful] "We'll have to interview them. Properly."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: Properly. With a serious voice. You do the serious voice. Nobody believes mine.
* [sincere] "It's your country. You decide who comes in."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[smile]: *Our* country. Founding citizens decide together. Which isn't written down either. You see the problem. Paperwork.
* [quiet] Hold out your hand for the shrimp chips, like a clerk waiting for a passport.
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    > He puts the bag in your hand with both of his. Daniel, watching, stands up straighter. Even Hyunjun stops chewing.
charlie: Applicant one. Daniel-hyung. Where are you from?
daniel: Seoul.
charlie: No, really.
daniel[thinking]: …The third stool at the claw machine. The one with the good angle.
charlie[smile]: Accepted. That's a real answer. Applicant two.
hyunjun: Wherever everybody's going after this. I'll be there. I'm already on my way.
charlie[smile]: That's not a place, hyung. That's a *plan*. …Accepted. It's a good plan.
daniel: So we're citizens.
charlie[thinking]: You're long-stay visitors. With full snack rights. Citizens is two. Citizens was an airport thing.
daniel[smile]: Snack rights was all I wanted. I don't even like paperwork.
> Hyunjun produces a pen from somewhere. Charlie turns over the nearest sheet of paper, which turns out to be page four of Songha's schedule for tonight.
you: Should we be writing on that?
charlie: Page four is "04:00 to 04:30 — contingency." This *is* the contingency. We're using it as intended.
> He writes at the top, in capitals, with the care of a man signing a lease: THE CONSTITUTION OF WHEREVER THE SNACKS ARE.
charlie: Article one. "Nobody leaves first."
hyunjun: What if I have a call to take?
charlie: Then you say so, and we all understand, and you come back. It's about the spirit of the thing, hyung.
charlie: Article two. "Loser buys. Winner still has to share." That's old law. That's from the airport.
daniel: Article three: "Daniel's jokes belong to Daniel, even the ones that used to be Charlie's."
charlie[smile]: That goes in the appendix. Under crimes.
> He holds the pen out to you, cap first.
charlie[smile]: Article three's yours. Founders take turns. Anything you want. It's a real law once it's on page four.
* [sincere] Write: "The one who saves the seats gets a seat saved too."
    @var charliex_article = 1
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    > He reads it upside down. Then he turns the page round and reads it the right way up, in case it says something different.
    charlie[thinking]: …That's a law about me.
    you: It's a law about whoever saves the seats.
    charlie: That's me. I'm the only one who does that. You wrote a whole law and it's got one person in it.
* [playful] Write: "First answers are accepted. 'No, really' is by invitation only."
    @var charliex_article = 2
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: You're regulating my best move.
    you: I'm protecting it. It's too powerful for general use.
    charlie[smile]: By invitation only. Okay. You're invited, by the way. Permanently. That's a verbal amendment.
* [quiet] Write: "One light stays on."
    @var charliex_article = 3
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > He doesn't say anything. He just taps the line twice with one finger, the way you'd knock on a door you already know is open.
    charlie[smile]: That's the whole country, in four words. I've been trying to explain it for years. You did it in four.
hyunjun: Do we get stamps? Real countries do stamps.
charlie[thinking]: …We don't have a stamp. That's a serious gap. We may not be a real country.
> And for the second time tonight, it's a strange thing, knowing the answer.
you: Front desk. Top drawer this time.
> It's a wooden stamp with a worn handle and an ink pad gone pale at the middle. Charlie breathes on the pad like it's a cold window, and tries it on the corner of page four.
@sfx click
> Faint, purple, perfectly readable: STUDIO 25 — VISITOR.
charlie[smile]: It works. Five years in a drawer, and it works.
> Then he reads it again, and his smile goes a little crooked.
charlie[thinking]: "Visitor." Hm. Well. It's a fine word. I've been a visitor in some very nice places.
> Daniel takes the pen off the sofa without a word. He holds out his own hand, lets Charlie stamp the back of it, and then writes two small words above the purple.
daniel[smile]: There. "NOT A VISITOR." Fixed. Took two seconds. You're welcome, nation.
charlie: …Hyung.
daniel: What? It was a typo. Buildings make typos.
hyunjun[smile]: Mine next. I'd like the amendment as well, if it's going.
> Charlie stamps Hyunjun. Charlie stamps Daniel's other hand, by request. Charlie stamps the shrimp chips, which makes it, officially, the flag.
> Then he turns to you with the stamp raised, stops, and turns it round so the handle faces you.
charlie: No. Wait. You do mine. I've been the one at the desk for every place I've ever lived. I'd like to be the one who gets let in.
* [sincere] Stamp his hand, and write the two words above it yourself.
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    > You take your time over the letters. NOT A. He watches every stroke, very still, holding his own wrist steady with his other hand.
    charlie[smile]: Your capital A is better than Daniel-hyung's. Don't tell him. I'll have to wash this off tomorrow. I'd like it noted that I won't enjoy it.
* [playful] Ask him, in the serious voice, "Purpose of your stay?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: Snacks. …No. Staying. The purpose of my stay is staying.
    you: Accepted.
    @sfx click
    > The stamp comes down. Daniel adds the two words before anyone can ask him to.
* [quiet] Hold his hand steady with yours while you stamp it. {if charlie.closeness >= 4}
    @rel charlie romance+1 closeness+1
    > It isn't necessary. He could hold his own hand still. Neither of you mentions this.
    @sfx click
    charlie[shy]: …That's a very thorough border. I'd come through it again. Slowly.
@hide hyunjun
@hide daniel
@show songha neutral at=l
songha: Why does page four of my schedule have a purple stamp on it?
charlie: It's a constitution now, hyung. It's gone up in the world.
> Songha reads it. All three articles. His face does several things, and settles on the one he uses for good weather.
songha[smile]: Article one is sound. I'll need a copy for the file.
@hide songha
> Charlie folds page four into quarters and tucks it into the front pocket of the tote, behind the earbuds, where the important things go.
charlie[smile]: We've got paperwork. If anyone asks where I'm from, I can show them a document. It's got a stamp and a crime appendix.
charlie: Best country I've ever lived in. And I've done research.
@goto charlie_ch3

# ------------------------------------------------------ CH3: THE EMPTY FIELD
=== charlie_ch3
@autosave
@bg studio_lobby t=cut
@music quiet
@amb room
@time 03:47
@show charlie smile at=c
@sfx echo
@textbox echo
echo: Owner Charlie. A small administrative matter, while there is time.
echo: Your record's label has one empty field. ORIGIN. Every other owner filled theirs in five years ago.
echo: You wrote: "ask me later." It is later. I am asking.
@textbox normal
charlie[smile]: I wrote "ask me later" on an official form. That sounds like me. I admire his nerve. I'd also like a word with him.
@textbox echo
echo: The field holds twenty-four characters. It will be printed on the card if your record is kept. There is no hurry. There is some hurry.
@textbox normal
> He's still smiling. But his hand has found the strap of the tote, and closed.
charlie: Twenty-four characters. Sure. Can we go somewhere with a mirror? I think better when I can see what my face is doing.
@bg studio_practice t=fade d=1.4
@amb night
> The practice room is dark except for the strip of light under the door and the long mirror holding on to it.
> Charlie sits down in the middle of the floor, cross-legged, facing his own reflection like it owes him an answer.
charlie[thinking]: Okay. Help me practise. You ask, I answer. Like the airport, except there's a character limit.
you: Where are you from?
charlie: Seoul. Five characters. Done. …No. That's the taxi one.
you: Where are you from?
charlie: The long one's about nine hundred characters. It's got dates in it.
you: Where are you from?
charlie[thinking]: …Between. Seven characters. It fits. It fits very well. I don't want it on a card.
> He lies back flat on the floor. The mirror shows two of him, both looking at the ceiling.
charlie: It's not the card. The card's fine. It's what comes after.
charlie: When we debut, there's a profile. Name. Birthday. And then a line that says where you're from. One line.
charlie[thinking]: And then interviews. Every single one. "So, Charlie, where are you from?" And I get four seconds, and there's a camera.
charlie: The napkin doesn't fit in four seconds. I've tried. I timed it.
* [sincere] "What do you want to say? If there were no limit."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[smile]: …That's the hard one. I was hoping you'd start with an easier one.
* [playful] "What's your best time on the napkin?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: Forty-one seconds. No breathing. The interviewer would have to be very relaxed.
    charlie[thinking]: …But that's not really the problem. The problem's what I'd say if I could.
* [quiet] Lie down on the floor next to him and look at the same ceiling.
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > The floor is cool and slightly sprung. From down here the room is mostly ceiling tiles, and one of them has a water stain shaped like a boot.
    charlie[smile]: I'd photograph that ceiling. Fourteen-year-old me would've loved that ceiling.
    > He's quiet for a bit. Then he says it to the boot.
charlie: I want to say "here." These seven. This.
charlie[thinking]: But that's a lot to put on people. It's like walking into someone's house once and calling it yours.
charlie: What if I say it on camera, and it's bigger for me than it is for them? They're *from* places. I'd be from *them*.
charlie: I've never said that out loud. It sounds heavier out loud. It sounds like I'm asking for something.
> You think of a charger handed over on day four. A seat at dinner. A pen taken off a sofa, and two small words written above a stamp.
* [sincere] "Daniel wrote NOT A on your hand twenty minutes ago. Nobody asked him to."
    @rel charlie trust+2
    > He lifts his hand off the floor and looks at it. The purple's already going soft at the edges. The two words are still sharp.
    charlie[thinking]: …He didn't even wait. He just took the pen.
    charlie[smile]: All right. That's evidence. That's fairly strong evidence, actually.
* [playful] "Songha asked for a copy of your constitution for the file. You're in the file."
    @rel charlie closeness+1 trust+1
    charlie[smile]: I'm in the file. There's no getting out of the file. Songha-hyung's files are forever.
    charlie[smile]: …Yeah. Okay. You don't file a visitor.
* [romantic] "You're allowed to ask for something. I'd like to hear you ask." {if charlie.romance >= 3}
    @rel charlie romance+1 trust+1
    charlie[shy]: …That's a lot to say to someone who's lying on a floor.
    charlie: Okay. Then I'm asking. I want to be from here. There. I asked. Nothing fell over.
> He sits up. Something has come unstuck. He pulls the tote into his lap and digs, and comes out with a paper napkin and the pen from the sofa.
charlie[thinking]: I'm going to do the napkin. Properly. I've never done it for someone who said they'd follow the arrows.
charlie: Can you stay while I do it? I don't need help. I just need somebody in the room.
you: I'm in the room.
@sfx pencil
> He smooths the napkin flat on the practice-room floor and starts to draw. No talking. Just the pen, and his breath, and the mirror doubling all of it.
> A small square with a bulb over it. An arrow. A slope with three quick lines of rain. An arrow. A long rectangle with a row of doors.
> He's fast. He's done this a hundred times. The arrows have a practised swing to them, like a signature.
* [curious] Ask about the square with the bulb.
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: The kitchen. It's always first. Everything else is measured from the kitchen.
    charlie: It's got a buzz now. I'm adding a buzz. …There. Three little lines. That's the official symbol for buzz.
* [curious] Ask about an arrow that loops back on itself.
    @rel charlie closeness+1 trust+1
    charlie[thinking]: Oh. That one. I went back somewhere, once, and it had got smaller. Same place. Just smaller.
    charlie[smile]: I still put it on the map. It's allowed to be small. It was a good size when I needed it.
* [quiet] Follow the arrows with one finger, without touching the paper.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > Kitchen. Hill. Corridor. A rectangle with a board full of tiny dashes, which can only be an airport. You trace the whole route, in order.
    charlie[smile]: …You're actually doing it. People look at the napkin. Nobody *travels* it.
> The pen reaches the edge of the last drawing, a narrow building with a box next to it that must be a laundromat. It stops.
> He holds it there. The arrow that should come next doesn't come.
charlie[thinking]: Huh.
charlie: Every arrow on this thing points away from somewhere. I never noticed. I've drawn it a hundred times, and every single one is leaving.
charlie[sad]: I don't know how to draw one that isn't.
> The room is very quiet. In the mirror, two people sit over a napkin as if it's a fire.
* [sincere] "Then don't draw an arrow."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[surprised]: …Oh.
* [quiet] Wait. Let him find it.
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    > You wait. It costs nothing, and he can tell it's being given. The pen tilts, thinks, tilts back.
    charlie[thinking]: …Oh. It's not an arrow.
* [playful] "What do maps put where you already are?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[thinking]: …A dot. They put a dot. "You are here." Of course they do.
> He puts the pen down on the little building and draws a dot. A single, solid, slightly too big dot, pressed hard enough to go through.
> It leaves a mark on the practice-room floor underneath. He looks at that too, and doesn't wipe it off.
charlie[laugh]: It's got a dot. A hundred arrows and not one dot, all these years, and now my map has one.
charlie[smile]: I couldn't have done that by myself. I'd have drawn another arrow and made a joke about it.
@flag charliex_napkin
charlie: ECHO? I've got my answer. It's ten characters. "SEE NAPKIN."
@textbox echo
echo: I cannot scan a napkin.
echo: …I have scanned the napkin. It is a very good napkin. ORIGIN: SEE NAPKIN. Fourteen characters remain.
@textbox normal
charlie[thinking]: Fourteen. Fourteen…
> He counts it out against his thumb. Twice, to be sure. Then he smiles at the mirror, and both of him look quietly pleased.
charlie[smile]: "(IT HAS A DOT)". With the brackets. That's fourteen exactly.
@textbox echo
echo: That leaves no room for the space between them. I will donate the space.
echo: ORIGIN: SEE NAPKIN (IT HAS A DOT). Saved. It is the only label in the archive with brackets. I find I am fond of it.
@textbox normal
@sfx chime
> Charlie folds the napkin along its old creases, then stops, and holds it out.
charlie: Would you carry it? Just till five. I always carry the map. I'd like to know what it's like when someone else has it.
> It weighs nothing. You put it in your coat pocket, the inside one, and he watches it go the way you'd watch someone onto a bus.
charlie[smile]: And for the interviews, I've got four seconds. "It's a long story. There's a napkin." That's two seconds. I've got time left over to smile.
@goto charlie_ch4

# ------------------------------------------------------- CH4: THE VISITOR LOG
=== charlie_ch4
@autosave
@bg studio_frontdesk t=fade d=1.4
@music studio
@amb room
@fx dust n=0.4
@time 04:05
@show charlie smile at=c
> The stamp has to go back in its drawer. You could leave it on the sofa. You walk it back anyway. Some part of you is still on shift.
> The front desk is smaller than it was. The lamp that kept winning is still here, bent at the neck, without a bulb.
charlie: So this is the desk. *The* desk. You sat here a whole winter and knew where everything was.
> He doesn't go behind it. He stands on the visitor side and rests his arms on the counter, the way people do when they're hoping you have time.
> The top drawer sticks, then gives. Under the ink pad's empty space there's a ring binder you'd forgotten existed.
> VOLUNTEER SIGN-IN. One line per shift. Name, time in, time out.
charlie[smile]: Is that you? All of it? That's the same "I MEAN IT" handwriting.
> It is all you. Tuesdays and Fridays, a column of your own name, getting quicker and lazier down the page as the winter went on.
> The last entry is a Friday in March. Time in: 15:58.
> Time out is blank.
charlie[thinking]: You didn't sign out.
you: I left that spring. I suppose I didn't come back for the last line.
charlie[smile]: So, technically, you've been on shift for five years. That's the longest you've stayed anywhere, and nobody thought to tell you.
* [playful] "I'd like my overtime in snacks."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: The country will pay. We have a treasury. It's the front pocket of my bag.
* [sincere] "I didn't forget. I skipped it on purpose."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[thinking]: …Yeah. I wondered. It's a very tidy blank. People who forget leave a messier blank.
* [quiet] Run your thumb down the column of your own name.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > Twenty-three times. You count without meaning to. It's more times than you've written it on anything since.
    charlie[smile]: You were here a lot. For someone who was leaving.
> He's waiting. Not pushing. He's just got his arms on the counter, like someone with all the time in a refunded world.
you: I never did the last day of anything. If you sign out, it's finished. If you just don't turn up, it's only stopped.
you: I have a lot of places that are only stopped.
charlie[thinking]: …Oh. We're the same person backwards.
charlie: You skip the goodbye so it isn't real. I say hello loudly enough that nobody notices I'm worried it's the last one.
charlie[smile]: Between us, we've got one whole normal person. Arriving at the front, leaving at the back.
@show hamin smile at=l
> A paper bag lands on the counter between you, quietly, the way a cat puts down a gift.
hamin: You two missed the convenience store run. There's kimbap. One's tuna, one isn't.
charlie[smile]: Hamin-hyung. How did you know we hadn't eaten?
hamin: Neither of you has had a wrapper in your hand since one o'clock. It's fine. Eat.
> He glances at the open binder, at the blank on the last line, and then at you. He doesn't ask. He just moves the bag an inch closer to your side.
hamin[smile]: The lounge light's on, by the way. Somebody left a note. It was nice to walk in on.
@hide hamin
charlie[smile]: …It worked. Somebody got back last and it was on. {{playerName}}. We made policy.
> He tears the tuna one open, reads the instructions on the wrapper as he always does, and gets it wrong as he always does. The seaweed comes off in his hand.
charlie: Every time. Eight counts, I can do. Three numbered tabs defeat me.
> You eat standing at the desk, either side of it. It should feel like a counter between two people. It feels more like a table that's a strange shape.
charlie[thinking]: Can I try something? It's my first-week thing. Except backwards, and for you.
charlie: When I get somewhere new, I do all this work so the place feels like a home. Names, snacks, the whole show. You've seen it.
charlie[smile]: You do the opposite. You get somewhere and you keep your coat on. You still haven't taken your coat off tonight. I noticed at about two.
> You look down. He's right. It's been on all night.
charlie: So. Staying has steps. I've watched people do it my whole life. I've taken notes. Step one: you leave something here on purpose.
* [sincere] "Then I'll leave my name. Properly."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > There's a pencil in the drawer. Of course there is. You knew where it was.
    charlie[smile]: Look at you. Straight to the pencils. Go on.
* [playful] "I'm not leaving you my coat. It's cold."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: Keep the coat. I'm not a monster. Something small. Something you'd have to come back for.
    > There's a pencil in the drawer. Of course there is. He sees you find it without looking, and smiles.
* [quiet] Take your coat off and hang it on the back of the desk chair.
    @flag charliex_coat
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    > The lobby air is cool on your arms. The chair takes the weight like it's been expecting it.
    charlie[surprised]: …Oh. I was going to build up to that. That was going to be step four.
    charlie[smile]: It looks good there. It looks like someone works here.
> The binder is open between you. The blank is still blank. The pencil is the right kind, soft, with a flat side worn into it.
* [sincere] Fill in the old line: time out, 04:12, five years late.
    @var charliex_log = 1
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > You write it small. 04:12. It looks ridiculous next to 15:58. It looks finished.
    > Then, on the next line down, in the first fresh ink that page has seen in years: your name. Today. Time in: 04:12.
    charlie[smile]: Out and back in. In the same minute. That's the fastest anybody's ever missed a place.
* [quiet] Leave the old line open, and write a new one under it.
    @var charliex_log = 2
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > Your name. Today. Time in: 04:12. The blank above it stays as it is.
    you: I don't think I ever left. I think I've been running late.
    charlie[laugh]: DELAYED. You were delayed. You and my door. The same thing, this whole time.
* [playful] Turn the binder round and hold the pencil out to him. "You're staff tonight. Sign me in."
    @var charliex_log = 3
    @rel charlie closeness+1 romance+1
    > He takes the pencil and turns the binder square to himself. He writes your name slowly, checks the spelling against the column above, and adds the time.
    charlie[smile]: I've never written your name before. It's a good one to write. I took my time on purpose.
> Under "time out," on tonight's line, there's a fresh blank.
> Charlie looks at it. His fingers tap the counter, once, twice, and stop.
charlie[thinking]: I'm going to say a thing, and it's not a rule. It's not article four. It's just a thing I want.
charlie: I don't want you to fill that one in.
charlie: There. I'm allowed to want it. I'm not asking you to promise anything. You can go wherever you go. I'd just like it on record that I didn't want you to.
* [sincere] "It's on record. I heard it."
    @rel charlie trust+2
    charlie[smile]: Good. That's all. I just wanted it heard. …That's easier than saving seats. Nobody ever told me you could simply say it.
* [romantic] "I don't want to fill it in either." {if charlie.romance >= 4}
    @rel charlie romance+1 trust+1
    > He doesn't answer straight away. He looks at the blank line, and then at you, and he holds the look longer than he has all night.
    charlie[shy]: …Good. Then it stays blank. Give me a second. I want to remember exactly where I was standing.
* [quiet] Put the pencil back in the drawer and close it.
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > No pencil, no line. He watches the drawer shut, and lets out a breath he must have been holding since the stairs.
    charlie[smile]: …Okay. Drawer's closed. Desk's closed. Best service I've ever had.
@show songha thinking at=r
songha: It's 04:16. I don't understand why it's only 04:16. I've redone the schedule. The extra time is labelled "free."
charlie[laugh]: Hyung, you scheduled the free time?
songha[smile]: Someone has to. Lobby at 04:57. I'm told the clock is sure this time.
@hide songha
> The binder goes back in the drawer, open at tonight's page. You leave the lamp as it is. It's won enough.
