# ------------------------------------------------------ CH5: EIGHT MINUTES
=== charlie_ch5
@autosave
@bg studio_ext_night t=fade d=1.6
@music quiet
@amb night
@fx off
@time 04:22
@show charlie smile at=c
> Kimbap needs something to drink. That's the whole reason you're outside. It's a good enough reason.
@if flag.charliex_coat
    > Your coat is still on the desk chair. At the door Charlie takes off his cardigan and holds it out, without a speech, which is how you know he means it.
    charlie: Step one was leaving the coat. Nobody said step two was being cold.
    > It's warm from him, and the sleeves are too long. The napkin has moved to your other pocket. You checked.
@else
    > The alley air is cold and rinsed. The napkin is still in your inside pocket. You've checked twice. He saw you check the second time, and looked away, smiling.
@sfx vending
> The machine by the door hums to itself. Charlie feeds it coins one at a time, like feeding something that might bite.
@if var.drink == "peach"
    > He presses the button for peach iced tea before you've said a word, and then one for himself.
@elif var.drink == "choco"
    > He presses the button for hot chocolate before you've said a word, and then one for himself.
@elif var.drink == "coffee"
    > He presses the button for black coffee before you've said a word, pulls the same face as before, and gets himself something sweeter.
@elif var.drink == "grape"
    > He presses the button for grape soda before you've said a word, and then one for himself. Both are aggressively purple.
@else
    > He presses two buttons without asking, and gets it right, because he watched what you picked the last time.
@sfx can
> The laundromat is open. It's always open. Behind the glass, under lights the colour of milk, one dryer in the long row is still turning.
> There's nobody inside. Just the one machine, and in it a single yellow blanket, going round, falling, going round.
> A red number glows on the dryer's door. 08.
charlie[thinking]: Somebody's coming back for that. They've gone to get something and they're coming back.
> There's a bench outside the window, bolted to the wall, with a view of the row. He sits at one end. He leaves you the end nearer the warm air from the door.
@if var.homeword == "smell"
    charlie[smile]: Laundry soap. Your answer. We've gone from the vent to the source.
@elif var.homeword == "people"
    charlie[smile]: "Whoever's still up." You, me, and a blanket. It's a small household, but it's awake.
@elif var.homeword == "place"
    charlie[smile]: It's a window. You said home was a window. This one's got a whole row of little round ones inside it.
@else
    charlie[smile]: This is the warmest bench in Mapo. I'd put money on it. Not a lot of money.
charlie: Okay. I've got a game. It's a bad game. It's got no points and nobody wins.
charlie[thinking]: Eight minutes. Till the dryer stops. I sit here and I don't fill it. No bits. No surveys. No hosting.
charlie: And you sit here and you don't plan the exit. I know you do. You've got your eye on the end of the alley right now.
> You have. You take your eye off it.
* [sincere] "Deal. I'm bad at it too. Let's be bad at it together."
    @rel charlie trust+1
    charlie[smile]: Together. We keep using that word. It's going to wear out. I hope it doesn't.
* [playful] "What happens if you talk?"
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: Nothing. That's the unnerving part. Nothing happens, and I find out the silence was fine all along.
* [quiet] Put your can down on the bench between you, and sit back.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > He puts his next to it. Two cans, touching at the rims. He nods at them like they've signed something.
> 08 becomes 07.
> The blanket goes round. It climbs the drum, hangs at the top for a second as if deciding, and drops.
> Charlie's fingers find the rim of his can and turn it, a quarter at a time. He notices. He sets the can down, lays his hand flat on his knee, and leaves it there.
> 06.
> A scooter goes by at the end of the alley, and its sound takes a long time to leave. Your eyes follow it to the corner. You bring them back.
> He noticed. He doesn't say so. He just shifts on the bench, a very little, so his shoulder is nearer yours. It isn't touching. It's close enough to feel the difference.
> 05.
> He takes his earbuds out of the tote and works at the knot. He doesn't play anything. He holds one out anyway.
* [quiet] Take it, and listen to nothing with him.
    @rel charlie closeness+1 trust+1
    > The cable's still too short. With nothing playing, the earbud just makes the dryer sound rounder and further away, like hearing it from another room in the same house.
    > He has his eyes closed. You have never seen him with his eyes closed.
* [curious] Raise an eyebrow at the silent earbud.
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    > He shrugs, and points at the dryer, and then at his ear. That's the song. Eight minutes, one instrument, no handclaps.
    > You take it. He's right. It's not bad.
* [romantic] Take it, and let your shoulder rest against his. {if charlie.romance >= 4}
    @rel charlie romance+1
    > He breathes in, and then out, slowly, like someone lowering a full cup onto a table. He doesn't move away. He doesn't narrate it.
    > It's the quietest thing he's done all night, and it takes all of him.
> 04.
> He holds out until then. It's the longest he's gone. When he does speak, it's low, and it isn't a bit.
charlie: I'm happy.
> He says it to the dryer.
charlie: That's talking, I know. It isn't filling. I checked. It's just true, and I didn't want to carry it by myself.
charlie: I'm not building anything right now. I'm not making it nice for anyone. I'm just sitting down, and I'm happy, and I didn't do anything to earn it.
you: You don't have to earn a bench.
charlie[smile]: No. I know. I'm finding that out. It's taking eight minutes.
> 03.
charlie[thinking]: Can I say the other thing? It's the back of the same thing.
charlie: When I leave places, I make it easy. I go out laughing. Big wave, big joke. So nobody has to feel bad. So nobody has to miss me.
charlie[thinking]: And it works. That's the thing. It really works.
charlie: I think I'd like to be missed. A little. I know how that sounds. It sounds like wanting someone to be sad.
> The blanket climbs, and hangs, and drops.
* [sincere] "I'd miss you. I wouldn't be any good at hiding it."
    @rel charlie romance+1 trust+1
    > He turns to look at you for the first time in five minutes. Whatever he finds, he keeps.
    charlie[shy]: …Okay. Then I've got one. That's all I needed. I only needed the one.
* [sincere] "It's not wanting someone sad. It's wanting to have been there."
    @rel charlie trust+2
    charlie[thinking]: …To have been there. Yes. Like the water stain shaped like a boot. Proof that something happened.
    charlie[smile]: I'd like to be somebody's boot. That's the worst sentence I've ever said, and I mean it completely.
* [quiet] "Seven people would notice in under a minute. I've watched them look for you."
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    charlie[thinking]: …They do look. When I go quiet, Hamin-hyung looks. I always thought he was just counting heads.
    charlie[smile]: He's counting *me*. …That's different. That's a different thing entirely.
> 02.
charlie: What about you? You do it the other way. You don't sign out. Nobody gets to miss you properly, because nobody knows you've gone.
> It's said gently. It's also completely accurate. He's put his hand on the exact place, the way he'd steady a chair.
* [sincere] "I think I was afraid nobody would."
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    charlie: I did. I sat in that airport three more hours. I ate the rest of the snacks and I was annoyed at a gate.
    charlie[smile]: That's missing someone. I was fourteen and I didn't know the word for it, so I blamed the board.
* [playful] "You missed me. You told me. You were annoyed at a gate."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[smile]: I held a grudge against that gate for years. I see a thirty-one and I still take it personally.
    charlie[smile]: So there. You've been missed since you were a teenager. It's already happened. You can't undo it.
* [quiet] Say nothing, and don't look at the end of the alley.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > You keep your eyes on the yellow blanket. It takes some doing. He watches you do it, and knows exactly what it's costing, and says nothing either.
> 01.
> Neither of you says anything for the last minute. It turns out to be easy. It turns out it was always going to be easy, with the right bench.
@sfx bell
> The dryer stops. The blanket settles in a soft heap against the glass. The red number goes out.
> But the light inside the drum stays on. A small, round, yellow room, lit for whoever's coming back.
charlie[smile]: Even the dryer leaves one on. It's policy everywhere. I didn't start it. I just joined.
> He stands, and stretches until something in his back clicks, and looks down the alley to the corner. Nobody yet.
charlie[smile]: They'll come. It'll be warm when they get here. I don't have to wait and check. …That's new. I usually wait and check.
> He holds the studio door, then catches himself, laughs, and lets go of it so that you both have to push.
@goto charlie_ch6

# --------------------------------------------------- CH6: WHAT BETWEEN LOOKS LIKE
=== charlie_ch6
@autosave
@bg studio_rooftop t=fade d=1.6
@music charlie
@amb wind
@fx stars n=0.4
@time 04:40
@show charlie smile at=c
> The crates are where you left them. So is the paint can. So is the vent, breathing out laundry soap for nobody.
> The sky hasn't started yet, but it's thinking about it. The dark over the river has gone thin, like a sleeve worn at the elbow.
charlie: I came back for a reason. I've got one thing left on the list. The list that said "everything."
> He takes out his phone and holds it up at the rooftops, the way he must have held one up at a departures board.
charlie[thinking]: The letter thing said: send one photo of something that matters. I sent "between." It was true. It was what I had.
charlie: I want to take the new one. What between looks like now. Nobody's asking for it. There's no booth. I just want it to exist.
@sfx click
> He presses the button. The phone makes a small, unhappy sound.
charlie[thinking]: "Storage full." Of course. Tonight of all nights.
> He turns the screen to show you. Four thousand three hundred and twelve photographs.
charlie[smile]: So. I never stopped. Ceilings. My shoes. Other people's dogs. There's a whole year that's just bus windows.
charlie[thinking]: Fine. I delete one. Just one. Help me pick a ceiling I can live without.
> He scrolls. They go by in a blur of off-white.
* [playful] "That one. It's beige. It has nothing going for it."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[thinking]: That's the hallway outside the practice room the day I passed my first monthly evaluation. I lay on the floor for ten minutes.
    you: …Not that one.
    charlie[smile]: Not that one. You see the problem.
* [curious] "What's the oldest one on there?"
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > He scrolls for a long time. At the very bottom: an amber board, a row of DELAYEDs, a hand in the corner.
    charlie[smile]: That's the floor of the whole phone. Everything else is standing on it. Can't delete the floor.
* [quiet] Watch them go past, and let him decide.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > A classroom ceiling. A ceiling with a fan. A ceiling seen through the fingers of a hand held up against a lamp.
    charlie[thinking]: …That's a Tuesday I liked. That one's a Tuesday I didn't like, but I got through it. I need that one more, actually.
> His thumb hovers over a photograph of a perfectly ordinary dog. It hovers for a long time.
charlie[thinking]: I can't. They're all somewhere I was. If I delete one, that day's only in my head, and my head moves around a lot.
> It would be easy to say "it's only a photo." It isn't. For someone from between, four thousand ceilings is a kind of address.
* [sincere] "Then don't delete anything. I'll take it. On mine."
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    charlie[thinking]: On yours?
    you: I'll send it to you when you've got room. Until then I'll hold it.
    charlie[smile]: …You'd carry my photo. You're already carrying my map. You're going to need a bigger coat.
* [playful] "My phone has eleven photos on it. Nine are parking signs. Use mine."
    @rel charlie closeness+1 trust+1
    charlie[laugh]: *Eleven?* What have you been doing with your life?
    you: Leaving before it got photogenic.
    charlie[smile]: Okay. We're fixing that. Starting with twelve.
* [quiet] Take your phone out and put it in his hand.
    @rel charlie trust+1 closeness+1
    > He looks at it. It's unlocked. There's almost nothing on it. He holds it carefully, the way you'd hold someone's keys.
    charlie[smile]: You just handed me your whole phone. That's more trust than the chair.
> He frames it three different ways. The river. The crates. His own shoes, out of habit. None of them is it.
> Then he goes to the edge of the roof, on the alley side, and looks down, and laughs once under his breath.
charlie[smile]: There. Look. {{playerName}}, look.
@cam pan
> Below, in the back wall of Studio 25, one window is lit. Small, square, slightly yellow. The kitchenette.
> Its light falls across the wet alley and meets the laundromat's halfway. Two lit rooms, holding the dark between them like a rope.
charlie: That's it. That's what between looks like now. It's got a light on at both ends.
> He holds the phone up. Then he lowers it again.
charlie[thinking]: It needs the hand. It's not the photo without the hand.
charlie: Would you? On purpose, this time. Last time you didn't know you were in it.
> You step up beside him and point. At the little yellow window. At a bulb with your handwriting on its box.
> And then, in the other corner of the frame, a second hand comes up. His. Pointing at the same place.
charlie: Future me should know there were two.
@sfx shutter
@flash
> The shutter is far too loud in all that quiet. It always was.
> The photo is a little dark and a little tilted. Two hands. One window. On your phone it's number twelve. On his, one day, it'll be four thousand three hundred and thirteen.
@flag charliex_photo
charlie[smile]: It'll move with both of us now. Every time either of us changes phones. It's got two homes. It's fine with that. It takes after me.
@if flag.charliex_napkin
    > The napkin is still in your pocket. You hold it out. He looks at it, and closes your fingers back over it.
    charlie: Keep it till five. You're good at carrying it. I like knowing where it is.
@sfx echo
@textbox echo
echo: Owner Charlie. Recipient {{playerName}}. I can see the photograph. I cannot store it. My shelves are closing.
echo: I would like to describe it to myself instead, while I can. "Two hands. One light. Nobody departing."
echo: That is the whole entry. I find it sufficient.
@textbox normal
charlie[smile]: "Nobody departing." …ECHO. That one got me. The card isn't even shiny yet. I've got nothing to blame it on.
> His eyes are bright. He lets them be, and breathes out, and you stand close enough that he isn't doing it alone.
* [sincere] "I'm glad I was on that roof. And in that airport. And in between."
    @rel charlie trust+1 romance+1
    charlie[smile]: Me too. All three. Especially between. I never thought I'd say that about between.
* [playful] "It's the dust. Roofs are dusty. It's a known fact."
    @rel charlie closeness+1
    charlie[laugh]: A known fact. Thank you. Finally, someone who respects the science.
* [romantic] Offer him your hand, palm up, and let him decide. {if charlie.romance >= 4}
    @rel charlie romance+1
    > He takes it without hesitating, and holds it properly. He looks at the two hands as if they're still in the photograph.
    charlie[shy]: Those were pointing at a window a minute ago. They've got range.
* [quiet] Stay beside him until he's finished.
    @rel charlie trust+1
    > It doesn't take long. It's not sadness. It's what happens when something that's been delayed five years finally lands, and has to taxi for a bit.
@time 04:55
@sfx echo
@textbox echo
echo: All recipients. It is 04:55. I have checked this against the laundromat's clock, which has no feelings. Please come to the lobby.
echo: I have one decision left, and it is not mine to make.
@textbox normal
charlie[smile]: That's us. For real this time.
> He shoulders the tote and looks at the stairs, and then at you.
charlie: Same as before. Together. Nobody first. It's article one now. It's the law.
> You go down side by side. It's still not efficient. On the landing, through the hall, you can see the lounge doorway, and it isn't dark.
charlie: Whatever happens at the wall. I walked in on a light tonight. Somebody held the chair. That's already happened. Nobody can un-happen it.
@time 04:57
@goto act4_choice

# ------------------------------------------------------------ FINAL: AT THE WALL
=== charlie_final
@bg studio_lobby t=cut
@music quiet
@time 04:57
@hide all
@cam pushin
> You say it, and the Archive Wall hears you. Box number four wakes up: one thin line of amber around its edge, waiting.
> The lobby goes quiet, as a room does when everyone in it decides to give two people a moment and pretends they haven't.
@show charlie smile at=c
> Charlie is next to you. He's got the strap of the tote in his fist again, the same as he did outside door four, a whole night ago.
charlie[smile]: You picked the airport. Out of everything. You picked the one with no dogs in it.
